@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@samp{org-linear.el} keeps the issues of one or more @uref{https://linear.app, Linear} teams in an Org file and syncs the two in both directions. You work on your issues with the Org commands you already use: change a TODO keyword to move an issue through its workflow@comma{} set a deadline for its due date@comma{} add a note to comment on it@comma{} refile it to move it to another project. Changes made in Linear@comma{} by you or by your colleagues@comma{} reach the file at the next sync.

Linear stays the authoritative copy and the file is a mirror. The package exists because the alternatives each give up half of that: Linear's own interface lives outside Emacs@comma{} and a one-way export cannot be edited. Unlike an export@comma{} @samp{org-linear} remembers what each issue looked like at the last sync@comma{} so it can tell which side changed a field and merge the two without overwriting either.

The package talks to Linear's GraphQL API through @samp{curl} and converts descriptions and comments between Markdown and Org with @uref{https://pandoc.org, pandoc}. Both run as subprocesses@comma{} so Emacs never waits on the network.

Its capabilities fall into four groups:

@itemize
@item
The file: how issues@comma{} projects and their fields appear in Org (@ref{The file, , The file}).
@item
Syncing: when a sync runs@comma{} how it merges@comma{} and what happens on conflicts (@ref{Syncing, , Syncing}).
@item
Setup: the API key@comma{} the teams and the mappings (@ref{User options, , User options}).
@item
The agenda: a single command that lists your open issues@comma{} and your deadlines among your other tasks (@ref{The agenda, , The agenda}).
@end itemize

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@samp{org-linear} requires Emacs 29.1 or later@comma{} Org 9.7 or later@comma{} and the @samp{curl} and @samp{pandoc} programs on your @samp{exec-path}.

With Elpaca:

@lisp
(use-package org-linear
  :ensure (:host github :repo "benthamite/org-linear")
  :custom
  (org-linear-teams '("ENG"))
  :config
  (org-linear-auto-sync-mode))
@end lisp

With Emacs's built-in @samp{package-vc} (Emacs 30 or later):

@lisp
(use-package org-linear
  :vc (:url "https://github.com/benthamite/org-linear")
  :custom
  (org-linear-teams '("ENG"))
  :config
  (org-linear-auto-sync-mode))
@end lisp

Then create a personal API key in Linear (Settings@comma{} Security & access@comma{} Personal API keys)@comma{} give it access to the teams you mirror@comma{} and make it available to the package (@ref{The API key, , The API key}).

@node The file
@chapter The file

The file is @code{org-linear-file}. When it does not exist@comma{} the first sync creates it with @code{org-linear-file-header} and fills it. Each project is a level-1 heading with a @samp{LINEAR_PROJECT} property@comma{} and each issue a level-2 heading under its project. Issues without a project go under a heading named ``No project''.

@example
* Website
:PROPERTIES:
:LINEAR_PROJECT: 3f1c...
:END:
** DOING [#B] Fix the broken signup form                  :ana:bug:
DEADLINE: <2026-11-12 Thu>
:PROPERTIES:
:LINEAR_ID: ENG-123
:LINEAR_UUID: 6b0e...
:LINEAR_URL: https://linear.app/acme/issue/ENG-123/fix-the-broken-signup-form
:END:
:LOGBOOK:
- Note taken on [2026-11-02 Mon 10:14] (Sam Lee) \\
  I can reproduce it on Safari only.
:END:
The form rejects valid email addresses with a plus sign.
@end example

