@node How fields map
@section How fields map

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Linear
@tab Org
@item Title
@tab The heading's title
@item Workflow state
@tab TODO keyword (@ref{Workflow states and keywords, , Workflow states and keywords})
@item Priority
@tab Priority cookie (@code{org-linear-priorities})
@item Assignee
@tab A tag for the person (@code{org-linear-user-tags})
@item Labels
@tab Tags
@item Due date
@tab @samp{DEADLINE}
@item Project
@tab The level-1 heading the issue sits under
@item Description
@tab The heading's body@comma{} converted from and to Markdown by pandoc
@item Comments
@tab Notes in the log drawer
@end multitable

The identifier@comma{} the issue's internal ID and its URL are kept in the @samp{LINEAR_ID}@comma{} @samp{LINEAR_UUID} and @samp{LINEAR_URL} properties. The sync maintains them; do not edit them.

Tags that name neither a team member nor a label are yours: the sync leaves them alone and never sends them. A label's tag is its name with every character Org does not allow in tags replaced by @samp{_}@comma{} so the label @samp{requested-by:peter} becomes the tag @samp{requested_by_peter}. A tag you add sends the label of that name that the issue's team can use; if several can@comma{} it is logged and not sent.

Linear issues have no start date@comma{} so by default @samp{SCHEDULED} is not synced: use it freely to defer an issue in your own agenda.

@vindex org-linear-start-date-label
A team can stand in for a start date with a @samp{Start: YYYY-MM-DD} first line in the description and a label. Set @code{org-linear-start-date-label} to that label's name@comma{} such as @samp{"deferred"}@comma{} and @samp{SCHEDULED} syncs both ways with the two of them. A date in the file then shows as @samp{SCHEDULED} alone: the line leaves the body@comma{} and the label's tag leaves the tags. Setting@comma{} moving or removing @samp{SCHEDULED} rewrites the line and sets or removes the label on Linear@comma{} and for an issue in triage it also snoozes the issue until the start of that date in your time zone@comma{} or ends the snooze. Nothing is written when you turn the option on@comma{} unless a synced heading already has a @samp{SCHEDULED} of its own: that date is sent.

Every @samp{SCHEDULED} on a synced issue is then visible to your team@comma{} and the label stays after the date passes@comma{} until someone removes the date. A Linear view that hides the label keeps hiding the issue until then.

@node Workflow states and keywords
@section Workflow states and keywords

Each issue's workflow state becomes a TODO keyword. By default the keyword depends only on the state's type (@code{org-linear-type-keywords}): triage issues are @samp{TRIAGE}@comma{} backlog issues @samp{BACKLOG}@comma{} unstarted ones @samp{TODO}@comma{} started ones @samp{DOING}@comma{} completed ones @samp{DONE}@comma{} and canceled or duplicate ones @samp{CANCELLED}. @code{org-linear-state-keywords} gives individual states keywords of their own@comma{} for instance a @samp{WAITING} keyword for a state named ``Waiting''.

When you change a keyword in the file@comma{} the sync sends it as the state @code{org-linear-keyword-states} names for it@comma{} or else as the first state of the issue's team that maps to that keyword@comma{} in Linear's order of state types and positions.

The file's own @samp{#+TODO} line@comma{} written from @code{org-linear-file-header} when the file is created@comma{} must list every keyword these options can produce. The default header marks @samp{CANCELLED} with @samp{@@}@comma{} so Org asks for a note when you cancel an issue; the sync posts that note as a comment@comma{} which records why the issue was dropped.

@node Working with issues
@section Working with issues

@findex org-linear-set-project
Edit issues with ordinary Org commands@comma{} then save the file; with @code{org-linear-auto-sync-mode} the save sends your changes (@ref{Syncing, , Syncing}).

@itemize
@item
To comment on an issue@comma{} add a note with @samp{C-c C-z} (@code{org-add-note})@comma{} or with @samp{M-x org-linear-comment}@comma{} which also works from the list of your issues. Notes attached to state changes@comma{} such as the one the default header asks for when you cancel an issue@comma{} are posted too@comma{} prefixed with the new keyword.
@item
To create an issue@comma{} add a level-2 heading with a TODO keyword under a project heading@comma{} with its description as the body. At the next sync it is created in Linear@comma{} in the project's team@comma{} and gets its @samp{LINEAR_ID}. Until Linear confirms the issue@comma{} the heading holds the ID it was sent with as @samp{LINEAR_PENDING_UUID}@comma{} so that an issue whose creation was not confirmed is found by the next sync rather than created twice.
@item
To move an issue to another project@comma{} refile it with @samp{C-c C-w}@comma{} or run @samp{M-x org-linear-set-project}@comma{} which also works from the list of your issues. Either way you choose among the teams' projects only@comma{} with ``No project'' to clear it@comma{} and the file is saved@comma{} which sends the move. With a prefix argument@comma{} @samp{C-c C-w} runs @code{org-refile} as usual@comma{} for instance to jump to a heading. An issue has to stay a level-2 heading: refiled by other means under another issue@comma{} it drops out of the sync@comma{} which adds it back as a second heading.
@item
To assign an issue@comma{} tag it with the person's tag; an issue has one assignee@comma{} so a heading with two people's tags sends no assignee and the sync log says why.
@end itemize

Deleting a heading does not delete the issue: it comes back at the next sync@comma{} with its description and comments. To drop an issue@comma{} cancel it.

An issue deleted in Linear leaves the file at the next sync. If its heading holds edits@comma{} notes or subheadings not in Linear@comma{} which can no longer take them@comma{} it moves with them to the archive file (see @ref{Closed issues, , Closed issues}) and the sync log says so; with no archive file it stays in the file.

@findex org-linear-browse
@samp{M-x org-linear-browse} browses the issue at point in Linear@comma{} in the file or the list of your issues.

@findex org-linear-visit
@samp{M-x org-linear-visit} visits @code{org-linear-file}.

@node Closed issues
@section Closed issues

The file holds every open issue of the teams and the issues closed in the last @code{org-linear-closed-retention-days} days. Older closed issues leave the file at the next sync@comma{} unless their heading holds unsent edits or notes; such a heading stays@comma{} keeps syncing its edits@comma{} and leaves once it has nothing unsent.

@vindex org-linear-archive-file
An issue that leaves moves to the archive file named by @code{org-linear-archive-file}@comma{} under a heading named after its project@comma{} with the properties Org's archiving adds (@samp{ARCHIVE_TIME}@comma{} @samp{ARCHIVE_OLPATH} and the like). Your subheadings and notes go with it. By default the archive is @samp{linear-archive.org} next to @samp{linear.org}: a @samp{%s} in the option stands for the name of @code{org-linear-file} without its directory and extension@comma{} and a relative name is relative to that file's directory. The sync creates the archive with the file's @samp{#+TODO} and @samp{#+PRIORITIES} lines and saves it before the file. While the archive's buffer has unsaved edits@comma{} or the archive changed on disk@comma{} closed issues stay in the file and the sync log says why; they move at the first sync after you save or revert it. Neither the kill ring nor the clipboard is touched.

@findex org-linear-archive-closed
@findex org-linear-archive
You need not wait for the retention period to end. @samp{M-x org-linear-archive-closed} archives every closed issue in the file at once@comma{} and @samp{M-x org-linear-archive} the one at point; in the file@comma{} under @code{org-linear-auto-sync-mode}@comma{} it replaces @code{org-archive-subtree} (@samp{C-c C-x C-s})@comma{} which it calls for a heading that is not an issue. Either way the sync remembers the issue and leaves it out of the file while it stays closed; if someone reopens it in Linear@comma{} it comes back. Neither archives an open issue@comma{} which the next sync would bring back@comma{} nor one whose heading holds edits or notes not yet in Linear: sync first. They save the archive and the sync state@comma{} and the file too unless it had unsaved edits of yours.

Set @code{org-linear-archive-file} to @samp{nil} to delete closed issues instead. A heading with subheadings of your own then stays in the file for good@comma{} and the sync log says so.

@node Syncing
@chapter Syncing

@findex org-linear-sync
@samp{M-x org-linear-sync} saves the file@comma{} fetches the teams' issues@comma{} merges them with the file and sends the file's changes to Linear. It returns at once; the work runs in the background and a message reports when it starts and how it ended. A background sync reports only changes@comma{} so it stays out of your echo area. The first sync converts every description and comment@comma{} so it can take a few minutes; later ones convert only what changed.

The merge is three-way. For each field the sync compares the file and Linear with the issue as it was at the last sync@comma{} kept in @code{org-linear-state-file}:

@itemize
@item
A field changed only in Linear is updated in the file.
@item
A field changed only in the file is sent to Linear.
@item
When both sides changed a field@comma{} Linear's value wins@comma{} and yours is recorded in the sync log.
@end itemize

Before each write the sync checks when the issue was last edited. If someone changed it in Linear after the sync read it@comma{} the write is refused and the issue is merged again at the next sync@comma{} so a change made in Linear is never overwritten. A write Linear rejects@comma{} for instance for an invalid value@comma{} is logged and not retried until you change what was sent; the sync's other writes still go through. A write that fails before Linear judges it@comma{} through a timeout@comma{} a server error or a rate limit@comma{} ends the sync instead@comma{} and the next sync tries it again.

A sync never changes the file while it has unsaved edits@comma{} and never applies changes if the file changed on disk since you visited it. The changes a sync makes clear the buffer's undo history@comma{} since undoing them would send a reversal of your colleagues' changes to Linear.

@findex org-linear-auto-sync-mode
@findex org-linear-file-mode
@code{org-linear-auto-sync-mode} is a global minor mode that syncs whenever you save or visit the file@comma{} and every @code{org-linear-pull-interval} seconds while the file has no unsaved edits. In the file's buffer it turns on @code{org-linear-file-mode}@comma{} which runs a sync after each save.

@findex org-linear-show-log
@samp{M-x org-linear-show-log} shows the sync log: conflicts@comma{} refused and rejected writes@comma{} issues not created@comma{} and errors. When a sync fails@comma{} its message points there.

@node The agenda
@chapter The agenda

@findex org-linear-my-issues
@vindex org-linear-my-issues-sections
The file holds every open issue of the teams@comma{} so it is not the place to see what is yours. @samp{M-x org-linear-my-issues} is: it opens an agenda of your open issues@comma{} with each one's project (in a column as wide as the longest project name) and due month and day@comma{} grouped into the sections of @code{org-linear-my-issues-sections}. By default those are triage@comma{} in progress@comma{} waiting and to do; a section whose keyword the file does not define is left out@comma{} and backlog issues are not shown. A due date that is today or past is shown in @code{org-imminent-deadline}@comma{} and one within @code{org-deadline-warning-days} in @code{org-upcoming-deadline}. Each call rebuilds the list@comma{} even with @code{org-agenda-sticky} on. From the list@comma{} @samp{t} changes an issue's status and rebuilds the list@comma{} so the issue moves to its new section and point lands on the next one. @samp{C-c C-w}@comma{} or any key on @code{org-agenda-refile}@comma{} moves an issue to another project (@code{org-linear-set-project}). @samp{.} opens @code{org-linear-menu} (in place of the agenda's jump to today@comma{} which a list of issues has no use for)@comma{} @samp{TAB} shows an issue's heading in another window@comma{} @samp{C-x C-s} saves the file@comma{} which sends your changes to Linear@comma{} and @samp{g} refreshes the list. Issues scheduled for a later day stay out of it until that day; every other issue is shown@comma{} whatever your @code{org-agenda-todo-ignore-*} options say. Killing the buffer of the file also kills the list@comma{} whose lines would otherwise lead nowhere.

@vindex org-linear-my-issues-sync
The list opens at once from the file and then syncs@comma{} unless the file has unsaved edits@comma{} and it is rebuilt with point on the same issue if the sync brings changes. Set @code{org-linear-my-issues-sync} to @samp{nil} to turn this off.

Add @code{org-linear-file} to @code{org-agenda-files} to see your issues' deadlines with the rest of your tasks.

@findex org-linear-agenda-skip
@vindex org-linear-hide-others-in-agenda
@vindex org-linear-agenda-keep-tags
@code{org-linear-agenda-skip} hides issues assigned to someone else. Set it as @code{org-agenda-skip-function-global} to apply it to every agenda view; issues with no assignee and issues assigned to you stay. So do issues with a tag in @code{org-linear-agenda-keep-tags}@comma{} @samp{nil} by default@comma{} which suits a team that labels work it shares@comma{} for instance with @samp{joint}. @code{org-linear-hide-others-in-agenda}@comma{} @samp{t} by default@comma{} turns the filter off without removing it.

@lisp
(setq org-agenda-skip-function-global #'org-linear-agenda-skip)
@end lisp

