@node Teams and file
@section Teams and file

@vindex org-linear-teams
The user option @code{org-linear-teams} lists the keys of the teams whose issues the file mirrors@comma{} such as @samp{"ENG"}. It is @samp{nil} by default@comma{} and a sync refuses to run until you set it.

@vindex org-linear-file
@code{org-linear-file} is the mirrored Org file@comma{} by default @samp{linear.org} in @code{org-directory}.

@vindex org-linear-file-header
@code{org-linear-file-header} is the text a new file starts with. Its @samp{#+TODO} line must cover the keywords of @ref{Workflow states and keywords, , Workflow states and keywords}@comma{} and its @samp{#+PRIORITIES} line the cookies of @code{org-linear-priorities}. Notes must go into a drawer@comma{} or they would count as part of the description: the default header's @samp{logdrawer} startup option sees to that@comma{} and you may drop it if your @code{org-log-into-drawer} is already set. Change the header before the first sync; afterwards@comma{} edit the file's header directly.

@vindex org-linear-state-file
@code{org-linear-state-file} holds each issue as it was at the last sync@comma{} by default @samp{org-linear/state.eld} under @samp{$XDG_STATE_HOME} or @samp{~/.local/state}. Deleting it makes the next sync treat Linear's values as the starting point. The sync replaces it whole on each write@comma{} so a write cut short@comma{} by a crash or a full disk@comma{} leaves the previous state intact.

@vindex org-linear-closed-retention-days
@code{org-linear-closed-retention-days}@comma{} 7 by default@comma{} is how many days a closed issue stays in the file before it moves to the archive (@ref{Closed issues, , Closed issues}). With 0 it does not stay: it leaves at the first sync that finds it closed@comma{} and an issue you close in the file leaves right after the sync that sends the change.

@vindex org-linear-pull-interval
@code{org-linear-pull-interval}@comma{} 600 by default@comma{} is the number of seconds between background syncs in @code{org-linear-auto-sync-mode}.

@node The API key
@section The API key

The sync reads your Linear API key at the start of each sync and forgets it at the end.

@vindex org-linear-api-key-command
@code{org-linear-api-key-command}@comma{} a command as a list of strings@comma{} prints the key. It runs asynchronously@comma{} so it suits a password manager's command line. It takes precedence over the next option.

@vindex org-linear-api-key
@code{org-linear-api-key} is the key itself or a function that returns it. When it is @samp{nil}@comma{} the default@comma{} the key is read with @samp{auth-source} for the host @samp{api.linear.app}@comma{} for instance from a line in @samp{~/.authinfo.gpg}:

@example
machine api.linear.app password lin_api_YOURKEY
@end example

@vindex org-linear-api-key-forget-command
If your command caches the key@comma{} set @code{org-linear-api-key-forget-command} to a command that drops the cached copy. When Linear rejects the key@comma{} the sync runs it and retries once@comma{} so a rotated key is read afresh.

The key reaches @samp{curl} on its standard input@comma{} never on its command line@comma{} where other processes could see it.

@node Mappings
@section Mappings

@vindex org-linear-type-keywords
@vindex org-linear-state-keywords
@vindex org-linear-keyword-states
@code{org-linear-type-keywords} maps state types to keywords@comma{} @code{org-linear-state-keywords} maps individual state names to keywords@comma{} and @code{org-linear-keyword-states} chooses which state a keyword is sent as (@ref{Workflow states and keywords, , Workflow states and keywords}). For example@comma{} a team with a ``Waiting'' state and a ``Rejected'' state might use:

@lisp
(setq org-linear-state-keywords '(("Waiting" . "WAITING"))
      org-linear-keyword-states '(("CANCELLED" . "Rejected")))
@end lisp

@vindex org-linear-priorities
@code{org-linear-priorities} maps Linear's priorities@comma{} 1 (urgent) to 4 (low)@comma{} to Org cookies; it is @samp{A} to @samp{D} by default. Linear's ``no priority'' is no cookie. A cookie not listed is sent as the priority of the nearest listed cookie above it@comma{} so with numeric priorities you can map only some numbers:

@lisp
(setq org-linear-priorities '((1 . "1") (2 . "2") (3 . "4") (4 . "6")))
@end lisp

@vindex org-linear-user-tags
@code{org-linear-user-tags} maps team members' names to tags. A member not listed gets the first word of their name in lower case@comma{} or their full name@comma{} as in ``peter@math{_hartree}''@comma{} when another member would get the same tag@comma{} so that a tag always stands for one person. Members of other teams have no tag; an issue assigned to one of them keeps that assignee@comma{} since the file cannot represent it.

@node Programs
@section Programs

@vindex org-linear-curl-program
@vindex org-linear-pandoc-program
@vindex org-linear-timeout
@code{org-linear-curl-program} and @code{org-linear-pandoc-program} name the @samp{curl} and @samp{pandoc} executables. @code{org-linear-timeout}@comma{} 60 by default@comma{} is how many seconds a request to Linear may take before it is abandoned and the sync fails.

@node Profiles
@section Profiles

@vindex org-linear-profiles
The options above make up the default profile. @code{org-linear-profiles} adds further ones@comma{} each with its own options@comma{} sync state@comma{} log and timer@comma{} so that one Emacs can sync with several Linear workspaces. It is an alist of @samp{(NAME . OPTIONS)}: NAME is a symbol and OPTIONS a plist whose keys are option names without the @samp{org-linear-} prefix. An option a profile leaves out takes its global value@comma{} except @code{org-linear-state-file}@comma{} which defaults to a file named after the profile beside the global one. For example:

@lisp
(setq org-linear-profiles
      '((personal :teams ("ME")
                  :file "~/org/personal-linear.org"
                  :api-key-command ("my-password-tool" "read" "linear-personal"))))
@end lisp

The default profile syncs when @code{org-linear-teams} is set; the others always do under @code{org-linear-auto-sync-mode}. @samp{M-x org-linear-sync} syncs the profile of the current buffer; with a prefix argument it asks which. @code{org-linear-show-log} shows the current buffer's profile's log@comma{} @samp{*org-linear: NAME*} for a named profile.

@node The in-place layout
@chapter The in-place layout

@vindex org-linear-layout
By default org-linear mirrors Linear into one file@comma{} with Linear as the primary copy. Setting @code{org-linear-layout} to @samp{in-place}@comma{} usually in a profile of its own (@ref{Profiles, , Profiles})@comma{} syncs TODO headings wherever they already are@comma{} across many files@comma{} with Org as the primary copy. A heading is linked to its issue by its @samp{LINEAR_UUID} property.

@vindex org-linear-directories
@vindex org-linear-exclude-files
@vindex org-linear-exclude-tags
@vindex org-linear-exclude-keywords
The headings synced are the open TODO headings in the @samp{.org} files under @code{org-linear-directories}@comma{} searched recursively@comma{} except those in files matching a regexp of @code{org-linear-exclude-files}@comma{} those with a tag in @code{org-linear-exclude-tags} (inherited tags count)@comma{} those with a keyword in @code{org-linear-exclude-keywords}@comma{} and habits. Exclusions apply to linked headings too: a linked heading that becomes excluded stops syncing@comma{} and its issue is left as it is. A linked heading you close in Org still sends its closing.

What a sync does:

@itemize
@item
It reads only the files that changed since the last sync or still hold headings to create@comma{} and fetches only the issues that changed in Linear since then. The first sync reads every file and opens those with TODO headings@comma{} which can take some seconds@comma{} so background syncs never make it: start it with @samp{M-x org-linear-sync}.
@item
A new heading in scope gets an issue. Before anything is sent@comma{} the heading gets the issue's ID as @samp{LINEAR_PENDING_UUID} and its file is saved@comma{} so an answer lost on the way back is found by the next sync rather than repeated. Once Linear confirms them@comma{} the new issues' IDs are written into their headings and each file is saved once@comma{} at the end of the sync. At most @code{org-linear-create-limit} issues are created per sync@comma{} and the rest wait for the next scheduled sync rather than an immediate one@comma{} so a first import spreads over several and keeps within Linear's hourly request limit. Each issue gets a label @samp{file:NAME} naming the file its heading is in@comma{} created in Linear when missing.
@item
Changes on either side are merged field by field@comma{} as in the mirror layout. When a field changed on both sides@comma{} @code{org-linear-conflict-winner} decides: set it to @samp{org} for this layout@comma{} and Linear's value goes to the sync log.
@item
An issue created in Linear goes under the heading @code{org-linear-inbox-heading} of @code{org-linear-inbox-file}. Refile it anywhere in scope and it keeps syncing.
@item
An issue closed in Linear sets its heading's done keyword; the heading stays. A heading with a repeater repeats instead@comma{} once for each time the issue was closed: the @samp{LINEAR_REPEATED} property records the closing it acted on.
@item
An issue deleted in Linear cancels its heading@comma{} which keeps its text and loses its link@comma{} so it is not created again.
@item
A linked heading that is no longer in any file under @code{org-linear-directories} (deleted@comma{} or moved out) archives its issue in Linear. A heading moved to an @samp{_archive} file still counts as present. If more than @code{org-linear-archive-limit} issues would be archived at once@comma{} as when a directory leaves the option@comma{} none is@comma{} and the log says why.
@item
A file with unsaved edits is skipped@comma{} and so is one edited while the sync runs: its headings neither pull nor push until it is saved. A buffer whose file changed on disk is reverted first if it has no unsaved edits. The sync never deletes heading text.
@item
Several keywords can stand for one Linear state@comma{} through @code{org-linear-keyword-states}; a heading keeps its keyword while its issue stays in that state. Likewise several cookies can stand for one priority@comma{} through @code{org-linear-priorities}@comma{} and a heading keeps its cookie while its issue keeps that priority. A keyword with no state is sent as the team's first unstarted state@comma{} or first completed one for a done keyword; a state whose keyword Org lacks arrives as Org's first open or done keyword.
@item
Assignees are left alone.
@end itemize

@vindex org-linear-merge-body-function
Linear rewrites the Markdown it is sent in its own style (bare links become @samp{[URL](<URL>)}@comma{} @samp{-} bullets become @samp{*}@comma{} trailing spaces go)@comma{} so the sync records the description as Linear stored it@comma{} in both layouts; the rewrite is never taken for an edit made in Linear.

A description edited in Linear replaces the heading's body when Linear's stored text of the old description converts back to exactly that body@comma{} so that the conversion loses nothing. Otherwise@comma{} because of markup Markdown cannot hold@comma{} such as a source block's header arguments@comma{} @code{org-linear-merge-body-function} merges the two: it gets the Org body@comma{} the Markdown of the last sync@comma{} Linear's new Markdown and a callback@comma{} and calls the callback with the merged Org body@comma{} or @samp{nil}. The result is used only if it says what Linear's text says@comma{} compared as Org. Otherwise@comma{} or when the option is @samp{nil}@comma{} the body stays and the log says why; that Linear text is not merged again@comma{} but a later edit on either side is. A model makes a good merge function.

@findex org-linear-preview
@samp{M-x org-linear-preview} lists@comma{} per file@comma{} the headings the next sync would create@comma{} and the files it would skip@comma{} without sending or writing anything@comma{} and closes again the files it had to open. Run it before the first sync.

A configuration might look like this:

@lisp
(setq org-linear-profiles
      '((personal :layout in-place
                  :teams ("ME")
                  :directories ("~/notes")
                  :exclude-files ("/checklists\\.org\\'")
                  :exclude-tags ("noexport")
                  :exclude-keywords ("PROJECT")
                  :inbox-file "~/notes/inbox.org"
                  :conflict-winner org
                  :api-key-command ("my-password-tool" "read" "linear-personal"))))
@end lisp

@vindex org-linear-create-limit
@vindex org-linear-archive-limit
@vindex org-linear-inbox-file
@vindex org-linear-inbox-heading
@vindex org-linear-conflict-winner

@node Limits
@chapter Limits

@itemize
@item
Editing or deleting a note that is already in Linear is not sent; only new notes are.
@item
Comment replies are not shown.
@item
An issue that re-enters the file@comma{} for instance after being reopened@comma{} gets its comments again from Linear@comma{} but your old notes on it are not restored.
@item
Markdown headings in a description become bold lines@comma{} since an Org heading inside an issue's body would split the issue.
@item
The sync reads up to 250 labels and 250 projects@comma{} and up to 100 comments per issue. A label beyond those 250 shows no tag@comma{} but editing the tags never removes it from the issue.
@end itemize

